  "query_preview": "..The proverbial calm before the storm gives us another \nrelatively mild and dry day today, then everything changes by late \ntonight and into Friday.\n\nA deepening long-wave trough over much of the eastern Pacific will \nbegin to draw sub-tropical moisture and remnant Monsoonal moisture \nnorthward by tonight. A weak s/wv around 30N will then move into \nArizona Friday morning, providing marginal vertical ascent, shear \nand instability. The instability will peak early, as lower-level \nmoisture moves into the central deserts, before fading by Friday \nevening as the column becomes saturated.\n\nThis moisture and associated forcing will bring about a 12-18 hour \nperiod of showers and thunderstorms to our area, especially to the \nsouth of the Mogollon Rim and into the foothills of Phoenix Metro. \nSome hi-res models indicate rainfall totals of over 2 inches by \nFriday evening, driven locally by training thunderstorms. Due to \nalready saturated conditions and recent flooding, we have issued a \nFlash Flood Watch for Yavapai and northern Gila counties on Friday. \nThis may need to be pushed northward, but we will focus on the \nhigher confidence regions for now.\n\nLater Friday night, the instability fades and weak subsidence \ndevelops behind the exiting wave. High moisture values will stick \naround into Saturday, but without the forcing and instability, we \nexpect just isolated showers.\n\nBy late Saturday evening, a significant tropical moisture plume \noriginating around 24N will take aim at Arizona, as the now fully \ndeveloped west coast trough starts to move inland. The main trough \nwill pick up another s/wv around 29N by Sunday morning, attempting \nto phase all this moisture and the s/wv with the main trough passage \nSunday afternoon.\n\nThis is a somewhat crazy situation and although there is still some \ntiming/phasing issues Sunday, confidence is growing for yet another \nmajor precipitation event. Additionally, the main trough will drive \na cold front through our area late Sunday and into early Monday. \nThis will provide additional lift, shear and instability, as well as \nlowering snow levels.\n\nThe QPF from early Sunday through early Monday will range from about \n1 to 3 inches. Locally higher amounts are possible over our southern \nCWA, closer to the tropical moisture plume. The increasing shear and \ncolder aloft Sunday afternoon may also lead to severe weather. It is \nquite likely we will need additional headlines for Sunday and Sunday \nnight, with flooding becoming a serious concern. Let us get through \nFriday first.\n\nFinally, high pressure returns from Tuesday onward next week, for \nwarming and drying conditions.",
